INTRO(2)                       P32W Unix                        INTRO(2)

NAME
       intro - introduction to system calls and error numbers

SYNOPSIS
       #include <errno.h>
       #include <sys/syscall.h>

       extern int errno;
       int __syscall(int n, int a, int b, int c, int d);

DESCRIPTION
       Section 2 describes the calls into the P32W Unix kernel. Most
       have a C library function of the same name, a one-line
       wrapper around __syscall; a few (pinfo, reboot) are reached
       only through __syscall itself, and a few related library
       routines (execv, execl, execvp, execlp, gtty, stty) are
       described with the call they use.

       A program enters the kernel with the SYSCALL instruction:
       the call number in r1 and up to four arguments in r2-r5.
       The kernel puts the result in r1 and preserves every other
       register. A failure comes back as the negative error number
       (-1 to -4095). __syscall(n, a, b, c, d) does exactly this;
       on a failure it stores the error number in the global
       variable errno and returns -1. errno is not cleared by a
       successful call, so test it only after a call has failed.
       An unknown call number fails with ENOSYS.

       The kernel checks every pointer it is given against the
       caller's page table; a bad one gives EFAULT, never a signal.

       DEFINITIONS
       Process. Each process has a process ID (1 to 2^31-1; init
       is 1), a parent, its own protected address space of 8 MiB
       (text from 0x1000, then data, bss and the heap that sbrk(2)
       moves; the stack grows down from 0x800000 by up to 512
       KiB), real and effective user and group IDs, a current
       directory, a file creation mask, up to 20 open files and at
       most one controlling terminal. The system has room for 64
       processes and 128 open files.

       File descriptor. A small integer, 0 to 19, naming an open
       file. 0, 1 and 2 are by convention the standard input,
       output and error. Descriptors made by dup(2) or inherited
       through fork(2) share one file offset.

       Path name. A string of at most 255 characters naming a
       file; one starting with / is looked up from the root,
       others from the current directory. Each component is at most
       28 characters. Every directory on the way must be
       searchable (x). An empty path names the current directory.
       A directory with a filesystem mounted on it stands for that
       filesystem's root; .. at a mounted root leads back out.
       There are no symbolic links.

       Permissions. A file has an owner, a group and nine mode
       bits (rwx for owner, group, others). If the effective user
       ID is the file's owner the owner bits apply; otherwise, if
       the effective group ID is the file's group, the group bits;
       otherwise the others bits. There are no supplementary
       groups. The super-user (effective user ID 0) passes every
       check, except that executing a regular file needs at least
       one x bit.

ERRORS
       The error numbers are Unix V7's; <errno.h> defines them.
       perror(3) and strerror(3) print them.

       1  EPERM    Not owner: the call needs the super-user or the
                   owner of the file or process.
       2  ENOENT   No such file or directory: a component of a
                   path name does not exist.
       3  ESRCH    No such process (kill, ptrace).
       4  EINTR    Interrupted system call: a signal came while
                   the process waited (pause, wait, a terminal or
                   pipe).
       5  EIO      I/O error on a disk.
       6  ENXIO    No such device: the device or its minor number
                   does not exist, or /dev/tty was used without a
                   controlling terminal.
       7  E2BIG    Argument list too long (exec).
       8  ENOEXEC  Exec format error: not a P32W a.out.
       9  EBADF    Bad file descriptor, or one not open for the
                   reading or writing asked for.
       10 ECHILD   No children to wait for.
       11 EAGAIN   No more processes: the process table is full.
       12 ENOMEM   Not enough memory, or no room left in the
                   address space (sbrk) or the mount table.
       13 EACCES   Permission denied.
       14 EFAULT   Bad address: a pointer argument is not mapped
                   (or not writable) in the caller's space.
       15 ENOTBLK  Block device required (mount, umount).
       16 EBUSY    Device or directory busy (mount, umount).
       17 EEXIST   The file exists.
       18 EXDEV    Cross-device link.
       19 ENODEV   The device cannot do the operation.
       20 ENOTDIR  Not a directory.
       21 EISDIR   Is a directory.
       22 EINVAL   Invalid argument.
       23 ENFILE   The system's open-file table is full.
       24 EMFILE   Too many open files in this process (20).
       25 ENOTTY   Not a terminal (ioctl).
       27 EFBIG    File too large: past 2^31-1 bytes.
       28 ENOSPC   No space left on the device (blocks or
                   inodes).
       29 ESPIPE   Illegal seek: lseek on a pipe or terminal.
       32 EPIPE    Broken pipe: a write to a pipe nobody reads.
       33 EDOM     Argument out of range (math functions).
       34 ERANGE   Result too large (library functions).
       36 ENAMETOOLONG
                   A path or a path component is too long.
       38 ENOSYS   No such system call.
       39 ENOTEMPTY
                   Directory not empty (rmdir).

       ETXTBSY (26), EROFS (30) and EMLINK (31) are defined but
       never returned.

